Nethack is a based on a mashup of myth, legend, folklore and religious
beliefs from any number of cultures, real and fictional. A goodly number
of these cultures have at their center stories which invoke themes of
betrayal.

IOW, acting terribly, flagrantly out of character at just the moment
it matters most is a perfectly legendary, mythical, saga-worthy thing
to do.  It isn't _nice_, but it does make for a good story. And
there's plenty of precedent for it; practically every body of myth has
at least one "trickster" or "Judas" figure.

Anyway, nethack is a puzzle game, not a role-playing game. You can
play it as an RPG anyway if that's what you want, but it's harder that
way.  You needn't be surprised when other players don't opt to take up
that particular challenge.

